  "note": "Level-1 replay labels for the 2026-11-07 monthly review (m05910). Each case is a reviewed expectation about what the shipped router would pick for one step class against one pool, decided before the run and then diffed against the code. The labels are rule-derived and hand-reviewed, not human ground truth: the point is that a future catalog, config or scoring change that moves any of these routes turns the test red, so the move is a decision instead of an accident. Coverage: 6 pools x 4 step classes = 60 cases; the original 20 pin cheap/strong scoring, the engineering and hard bonuses, effort ladders with and without every requested word, a strong-only pool that must still route cheap steps, and a relay/official tie that the official entry wins. The 40 added cases pin the step signals replay now restores: 16 vision steps (hasImage hard-filters untagged entries and moves the pick off the cheap untagged default), 12 channel cooldown cases (a blocked channel sorts behind its healthy sibling and an all-blocked pool still routes), and 12 escalation/preferCheap cases (evidence bonuses cap at two escalations, and the cheap preference outranking the hard bonus is pinned as designed). Pool entries only ever use the two tier words the router understands (cheap/strong): normalizePool coerces anything else to cheap, so writing \"standard\" would have implied a third tier that does not exist.",
